# bat configuration -- https://github.com/sharkdp/bat#configuration-file
#
# One command line argument per line, exactly as you would pass it on the
# command line. Blank lines and # comments are ignored. Anything here can be
# overridden per invocation by passing the flag again.

# The oxide theme, in bat/themes/oxide.tmTheme. Note that bat addresses a theme
# by its filename, not by the name recorded inside the file, so this is "oxide"
# rather than "Oxide" -- the latter is silently ignored with a warning and the
# default theme is used instead.
#
# bat serves themes from a binary cache rather than from disk, so this line does
# not work until "bat cache --build" has been run at least once, and it fails
# loudly rather than quietly: until then every single bat invocation prints
# "[bat warning]: Unknown theme 'oxide', using default." That warning is the
# expected state of a fresh install, and building the cache is the whole fix.
# Re-run the build after any edit to the theme, too.
--theme="oxide"

# oxide uses italics to carry meaning rather than decoration: comments and
# blockquotes are italic, and italic is the only thing separating the receiver
# (self in Python, this in JS) from an ordinary parameter now that they share a
# colour. bat suppresses italics unless asked, because not every terminal
# renders them, so the theme needs this to say everything it means.
--italic-text=always
